Highly durable cellulose nanofibrous composite membranes were prepared by in-situ growing of zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF-8) as spacers in the presence of TEMPO oxidized cellulose nanofibers (TOCN) as their anchoring points. The obtained composite membranes showed three-dimensionally networked nanofibers with ZIF-8 to generate porous structures, which gave high durability without critical compaction of the membrane under pressure $(1\sim3$ bar). The $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thick ZIF-8/TOCN membrane showed most superior water flux (84 $\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1})$ without critical flux drop for $24\mathrm{h}$ operation. Interestingly, the composite membrane exhibited highly selective removal of cationic dyes in the presence of anionic dyes due to strong interaction through negatively charged TOCN networks. The experimental results in the study reveal a novel strategy for durable cellulose nanofibrous membrane via introduction of metal organic frameworks for highly selective filtration.
## 1. Introduction
Water pollution is one of the most serious problems causing environmental crisis. Various technologies have been developed to effectively purify water via chemical, biological, and electromagnetic processes (Chong, Jin, Chow, & Saint, 2010; Droste & Gehr, 2018; Eccles, 1999; Gupta, Ali, Saleh, Nayak, & Agarwal, 2012). Among the methods, filtration process using polymeric membrane is widely utilized because of its high efficiency, relatively low energy consumption and excellent usability (Geise et al., 2010). The membranes made of synthetic polymers such as polysulfone, polyethersulfone, or polyvinylidene fluoride, are widely used for microfiltration (MF) and ultrafiltration (UF) applications (Zeman & Zydney, 2017). These synthetic polymeric membranes are generally consisted of asymmetric structures, which are often fabricated by non-solvent induced phase separation (NIPS) using large quantities of hazard solvents, e.g. $N\mathrm{.}$ -methylpyrrolidone (NMP), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF), dimethylacetamide (DMAc) (Figoli et al., 2014). This type of membranes may cause environmental problems during fabrication and after disposal due to low biodegradability and recyclability (Song et al., 2014; Stein, 1992).
Therefore, new types of eco-friendly materials and manufacturing methods are required for a sustainable development.
Recently, application of cellulose nanofiber (CNF) for membrane takes growing interest as naturally occurring material with its intrinsic and structural advantages, including sustainability, hydrophilicity, excellent mechanical properties and continuously interconnected pores (M. Park, Shin, Cheng, & Hyun, 2017; Voisin, Bergström, Liu, & Mathew, 2017; Zhan et al., 2018; Zhang, Feng, Wang, Jia, & Yao, 2018). Furthermore, TEMPO (2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxylradical) oxidized cellulose nanofiber (TOCN) has been mass-produced over 500 tons per year since 2015 (Isogai, 2018). The mass production of TOCN has triggered numerous researches and developments for cellulose nanofibrous membranes. Previous researches successfully demonstrated the porous structure of cellulose membranes could be obtained by preparing an ultra-thin layer ${\displaystyle(\leq1\upmu\mathrm{m})}$ (Gopakumar et al., 2017; Ma, Burger, Hsiao, & Chu, 2014; Zhang, Deng, Soyekwo, Liu, & Zhu, 2016). However, due to low loading amount, the ultra-thin layer of cellulose nanofibers limited the absorption capacity for positively charged molecules among the inherent properties of cellulose nanofibers (Chen et al., 2014; Gurgel, Junior, de Freitas Gil, & Gil, 2008; Ma,
Hsiao, & Chu, 2011). In addition, when the CNF is prepared to a film form i.e. a membrane, there is more chance for the nanofibers to be laid upon the pores made below, blocking the pores to flow water. Therefore, it is a challenging issue for the cellulose nanofibrous membrane to prepare a thick layer with maintaining a pore structure under a hydraulic pressure.
There have been several reports on the thick layer $(>10\upmu\mathrm{m})$ of cellulose nanofibrous membrane with the high flux by compositing with the particles. S. Varanasi et al. reported that a $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thick cellulose nanofiber membrane showed water flux of $55~\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ when silica nano-particle was embedded (Varanasi, Low, & Batchelor, 2015). M. Hassan et al. fabricated a $13\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thick CNF membrane with high water flux as $15.8~\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ of by embedding activated carbon (Hassan et al., 2017). The particles in the CNF membrane provided space and supported the structure to prevent the compaction of the membrane. However, those particles need additional immobilization in the membrane due to weak interaction between the particle and the cellulose membrane. Furthermore, there was few studies reported on the structural control of cellulose nanofiber for the further performance improvement using interaction of embedding nano-particle.
The aim of the current work is to develop a nano-structured CNF membrane via embedding a nano-particle. We fabricated the cellulose nanofibrous membrane with high durability by introduction of zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8). ZIF-8 $\mathrm{(Zn(mIM)_{2}}$ , $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{IM}=2$ -methylimidazole), a rigid nano-particle with positive surface charge, was grown at CNF via in-situ synthesis (Pan, Liu, Zeng, Zhao, & Lai, 2011; Zhu, Yang, Cranston, & Zhu, 2016). In the prepared composite membrane, the in-situ synthesized ZIF-8 was enveloped by the CNF due to the strong charge interaction between the ZIF-8 and the cellulose nanofibers, providing more porous and durable structure. It enabled to prepare thick cellulose nanofibrous UF membrane $(20\upmu\mathrm{m})$ with 84 $\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ of the flux after $24\mathrm{h}$ filtering, showing excellent durability against the applied pressure. The increase of the membrane thickness also revealed extraordinary selectivity in dye separation. To best our knowledge, this is the first work on the selective dye removal by filtration using the cellulose nanofibrous membrane.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Tempo-oxidized cellulose nanofibers (TOCN) suspension $(1~\mathrm{wt\%}~\$ in water, carboxylate content: $1.7\quad\mathrm{mmol}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , average diameter: $11.2\ \pm\ 2.4\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ) was purchased from ANPOLY (Republic of Korea). Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $98\%$ ), 2-methylimidazole $(99\%)$ , Janus Green B (J.G.B, $65\%$ dye contents), Orange G (Or.G, $80\%$ dye contents), Bromothymol blue $95\%$ dye contents), Vitamin B12 $(98\%)$ , Methyl Orange $(85\%$ dye contents), and Methylene Blue $82\%$ dye contents) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich Co.. The chemical structure of the dyes was described in Figure S5. All chemical was used without any further treatment. Deionized water (Milli-Q Millipore $18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) was used in all experiments.
## 2.2. General in-situ synthesis of ZIF-8 in TOCN dispersion
For preparation of composite membrane with various contents of ZIF-8, $\mathbf{\nabla}\times\mathbf{g}$ of TOCN aqueous suspension $.1\mathrm{wt}\%$ in water) was diluted to (150-X) $g$ of deionized water and dispersed for $10\mathrm{min}$ by bath-sonication. Depending on the CNF contents in the diluted TOCN suspension, zinc nitrate hexahydrate (2 eq to carboxylic acid contents) was added to each amount for $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ exchange. The detail conditions for in-situ synthesis are summarized in Table 1. After the mixture was gently agitated for $5\mathrm{min}$ , it was immediately gelated. The zinc ion absorbed TOCN (Zn-TOCN) was purified using centrifuge at $5000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature with distilled water. The procedure was repeated three times. The $Z\mathbf{n}$ content of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TOCN was $0.85\mathrm{mmol}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , indicating that the half of the carboxylate contents of the TOCN $(1.7\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\dot{}\dot{}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ) was stoichiometrically conjugated. The obtained ZnTOCN was dispersed in deionized water $\mathrm{(20mL)}$ by bath-sonication and then poured into a $50\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave. Following the previously reported recipe, the $\boldsymbol{0.41\ g}$ of zinc source $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ (1 eq) and $7.9\mathrm{g}$ of organic ligand (2-methylimidazole) $(70~\mathrm{eq})$ were dissolved in $10\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and mixed with the TOCN suspension (Pan et al., 2011). The prepared mixtures in the autoclave were kept at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ for in-situ synthesis of ZIF-8 in TOCN. The synthesized ZIF-8/TOCN was purified by centrifuge at $5000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and repeated until the supernatant was clear. The ZIF-8/TOCN composites were dispersed in distilled water at a concentration of $2{\ g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ with respect to the loaded TOCN.
## 2.3. Membrane fabrication
Cellulose nanofibrous composite membrane was fabricated by deadend filtration (Amicon 8050, Millipore, USA) upon quantitative filter paper (filter paper No.6, Advantec, Japan) under the applied pressure of 1 bar, so called nanopaper methods (Henriksson, Berglund, Isaksson, Lindstrom, & Nishino, 2008). Prior to the loading on filter paper, the ZIF-8/TOCN dispersion was weighed various loading amounts to control the thickness of the membrane, diluted in $50\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water and gently stirred. For durability test, the loading amount of the all ZIF8/TOCN composite membranes was fixed to $30\ \mathrm{gsm}$ (grammage, ${\mathrm{g/m}}^{2}.$ ) with respect to the TOCN. By the same procedure, 21-TOCN was fabricated at 3.75, 7.5, 15, and 30 gsm for the test of dye rejection.
## 2.4. Characterization
## 2.5. Membrane performance
The pure water flux of $x$ -TOCN composite was evaluated by filtering deionized water through the membrane by dead-end filtration (Amicon 8050, Millipore, USA). The effective permeation area (A) was $13.4\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in dead-end flow filtration system under 1–3 bar at room temperature. The water flux of the prepared membrane was calculated by measuring the amount of the collected permeate (ΔV) for fixed time $\mathrm{(\Delta\Deltat)}$ and fixed pressure (1–3 bar) as following equation.
$$
J_{\mathrm{w}}\left(\mathrm{LMH}=\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\right)=\Delta\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{A}^{-1}{\cdot}\Delta\mathrm{t}^{-1}
$$
The rejection ratio of organic dye was evaluated by filtering of organic dye solution $(10\mathrm{ppm})$ . The rejection ratio of organic dye was calculated from the difference of absorbance (A) of feed and permeate at UV–vis spectroscopy.
$$
\mathrm{R}=(1-\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{p}})\cdot\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{f}}^{-1}{\cdot}100\ (\%)
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
Table 2 summarizes the weight percent of the in-situ synthesized ZIF-8 in the composite membranes. The ZIF-8 fraction was obtained by subtracting the amount of pre-adsorbed Zn ion (noted as $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TOCN) from the total $Z\mathbf{n}$ content of the composite membranes. The net ZIF-8 contents in the membranes were roughly calculated from the ratio of the ZIF-8 fraction against the fraction of the synthesized ZIF-8 without TOCN. The in-situ synthesized ZIF-8 in TOCN was obtained as 10, 21, 45 and $56\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The sample code was simply named after the ZIF-8 contents. For example, 10-TOCN indicates that the composite membrane contains $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ of the ZIF-8.
The X-ray diffractions of the ZIF-8, the TOCN and the ZIF-8/TOCN composite membranes are shown in Fig. 1(a). The characteristic peaks at $2\Theta=7.3^{\circ}$ (011), $10.4^{\circ}$ (002), $12.7^{\circ}$ (112), $14.7^{\circ}$ (022), $16.4^{\circ}$ (013), and $18^{\circ}$ (222) were associated to ZIF-8 nanocrystals (K. S. Park et al., 2006). The pristine TOCN showed the peaks of typical cellulose I at
$2\Theta=14.8^{\circ}$ $(1\ \overline{{1}}\ 0)$ , $16.3^{\circ}$ (110) and $22.6^{\circ}$ (200) (Terinte, Ibbett, & Schuster, 2011). The peaks in the ZIF-8/TOCN membranes showed the mixed patterns of the ZIF-8 and the cellulose I crystalline without giving new peaks. Apparently, the characteristic peaks of the ZIF-8 in the composites became larger as the contents of the ZIF-8 increased. The ZIF-8 in the TOCN was successfully in-situ synthesized into the nanocrystal of metal organic framework. The FT-IR spectra in Fig. 1(b) showed the chemical structures of the ZIF-8, the TOCN, and the ZIF-8/ TOCN membranes. The FT-IR pattern of the composite membrane showed a mixed pattern of the ZIF-8 and the cellulose. The ZIF-8 characteristic peaks at $1574\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1145\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (corresponding to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-N}}$ stretching, respectively) in the composite membrane became more intense with increasing the ZIF-8 contents (Sun et al., 2012). As coincident to the XRD study, there was no new generations of the peaks observed in the FT-IR spectra of the composite membranes. It is clear evidence of successful synthesis of ZIF-8 in the TOCN without side reaction.
The zeta potential and the particle size of the dilute dispersion of ZIF-8/TOCN in water were measured to study the interaction between the ZIF-8 and the TOCN. The concentration of the ZIF-8/TOCN was kept as $100\mathrm{ppm}$ with respect to the TOCN for preventing the precipitation during the measurements. As shown in Fig. 2(a), the zeta potential of the ZIF-8/TOCN dispersion was increased from negative to positive with the contents of the ZIF-8 due to the opposite charge of TOCN and ZIF-8. Even in the dilute dispersions, the enveloped ZIF-8 was gradually aggregated due to the decrease of zeta potential. However, this strong ionic bonding between ZIF-8 and TOCN was the driving force to envelop the TOCN around the ZIF-8.
As shown in Fig. 2(b), the size distribution of the dispersion had bimodal distribution. The modal peaks in larger size of the composite dilute dispersions were corresponding to the TOCN, while the modal peaks in smaller size were corresponding to the ZIF-8 enveloped by the TOCN. This average size of the enveloped ZIF-8 in the dilute solutions was increased as the higher content of ZIF-8. Due to the in-situ synthesized ZIF-8 in composites had the statically identical size, the modal peaks in smaller size was attributed by the aggregation of the ZIF-8 enveloped TOCN. The size of ZIF-8 in the composites was statistically analyzed by a one-way ANOVA of the particle size obtained from TEM images (Fig. S1). The P-value was computed as 0.19 which is much higher than the significance level of 0.05. Based on the analysis, we failed to reject the null hypothesis that the mean diameters of the ZIF-8 in the composites was identical. The strong interaction between ZIF-8 and TOCN in dispersion state lead to control the structure of nanofiber along the contents for the membranes.
Fig. 3 shows the surface SEM images of the pristine ZIF-8, the TOCN and the ZIF-8/TOCN membranes. The pristine ZIF-8 had the typical morphology of cubic metal organic frameworks with the average diameter of $70\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in the figure, the cellulose nanofibers were randomly distributed. The surface images of the ZIF-8/TOCN membranes were significantly depending on the ZIF-8 weight percent. In 10- TOCN, the ZIF-8 was mostly buried in the TOCN, resulting in film-like surface. However, the composite membrane of 21-TOCN had porous structure, in which, the ZIF-8 was wrapped and interconnected by TOCN. In the three-dimensional networking of TOCN, the ZIF-8 in the composite membrane provided spacious pore and build robust network structure by anchoring the cellulose nanofibers. This networking structure could afford the high flux without significant decrease of the flux by operation time. The composite membranes with the ZIF-8 contents higher than $45\mathrm{wt\%}$ had porous structure with the TOCN enveloped the ZIF-8 but the membrane could not maintain the network structure.
Fig. 4(a) shows the change of the pure water flux along time for the membranes of the TOCN, Zn-TOCN and ZIF-8/TOCN composites under 1 bar of nitrogen pressure. Comparing with the composite membranes, the pristine TOCN and the $Z\mathbf{n}$ -TOCN had significantly low values of the pure water flux as 6 and $11~\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition, the blend composite membrane with $21\mathrm{wt\%}$ of ZIF-8 in Zn-TOCN without in-situ synthesis of ZIF-8 showed the poor flux of $11~\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ as like Zn-TOCN membrane (see Fig. S2). Because the ZIF-8 in the blend membranes was not grown at the cellulose nanofibers, the interaction of the nanofiber and the ZIF-8 was not enough to change the structure of nanofibers. The all composite membranes showed much higher flux than the pristine TOCN. The ZIF-8 particle played an important role of a spacer and an anchoring point between the cellulose nanofibers, maintaining the porous structure under given pressure and water flow. The water flux drop was still significantly depending on the amount of the ZIF-8 in the composite membranes. To evaluate the flux decrease with time, the ratio of the flux drop $\mathrm{(FD=(F_{0}-F_{24h})/F_{0}\bullet100(\%))}$ was simply calculated from the initial flux $(\mathrm{F}_{0})$ and the flux after $24\mathrm{h}$ $\left(\mathrm{F}_{24\mathrm{h}}\right)$ . The FDs of the 10, 21, 45 and 56-TOCN were obtained as 37, 29, 46 and $80\%$ , respectively. It indicates that the 21-TOCN more stably maintain the networked structure against the applied pressure and the water flow than the others. This good durability was mainly enhanced by the threedimensional interlinking of the nanofibers around the ZIF-8. Therefore, the further membrane performance was characterized using 21-TOCN. The flux-time curves of the 21-TOCN membranes with various thickness controlled by the loading amounts were shown in Fig. 4(b). As expected, the thinner membrane showed the higher water flux. The thickness change along the loading amounts of 21-TOCN was analyzed from the cross-sectional SEM images. The thickness had linear relationships to the loading amounts (see Fig. S4). However, the flux drop was not significantly influenced by the membrane thickness. Even though the loading amount was changed from 3.75 to $30~\mathrm{gsm}$ , the FDs remained as $31.7\%$ with standard deviation of 3.2. It indicates the membranes fabricated at different loading levels kept the equivalent porous structure regardless of their thickness. Depending on the loading level of the 21-TOCN membrane, the water flux was linearly increased with the applied pressure (Fig. 4(c)). It also means that the composite membrane stably maintained the permeability and the porous structure against the hydraulic pressure regardless of their thickness.
Fig. 5 shows the cationic dye removal performance of the 21-TOCN membrane via filtration. The rejection rate of Janus Green B was increased with the increase of the loading amount of the 21-TOCN. The rejection rate reached higher than $99\%$ for the membrane loaded with
$30\ \mathrm{gsm}$ (Fig. 5(b) and (c)). This capability was also visually confirmed with naked eye as illustrated in Fig. 5(a). As well known, cellulose nanofiber is cable of absorbing cationic dye due to the surface charge (Gopakumar et al., 2017). After the introduction of ZIF-8, the cellulose nanofibers effectively retained the porous structure as a membrane even in the thick state. It maximized the inherent absorbing capacity of cationic dyes, giving the membrane the high rejection rates with the high flux. Moreover, 21-TOCN exhibited promising cycling behavior without flux drop and the rejection rate stayed over $97\%$ after four cycles (see Fig. 6(b)). The structural durability provides remarkable reusability after simple washing by alcohol. Fig. 6(d–f) display the dye selectivity of the 21-TOCN membrane by filtering the mixed solution of Janus Green B and Orange G. Surprisingly, the membrane rejected only the cationic dye with the rejection rate higher than $99\%$ from the mixed dye solution while most of the anionic dye passed the membrane. The composite membrane also can be used repeatedly for the selective removal of cationic dye without critical performance reduction.
Table 3 exhibits the flux and the rejection rate of the composite membrane according to the various the organic dyes with different charges and molecular weights via continuous filtration. 21-TOCN membrane loaded with $30\ \mathrm{gsm}$ showed the high flux $(>85\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ regardless of the charge and the molecular weight of the dyes, while the rejection rate of 21-TOCN on the organic dyes was significantly influenced by the charge of the dyes. The neutral dyes without any charges such as Vitamin B12 and Bromothymol blue were scarcely rejected by 21-TOCN membranes due to the absence of charge interactions with TOCN or ZIF-8. The rejection rate of the cationic dyes remained higher than $80\%$ during $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ operation. Janus Green B was more efficiently removed by 21-TOCN membrane than Methylene blue probably due to the molecular weight effects. For the anionic dyes such as Orange G and Methyl Orange, their rejection rates were much lower than the cationic dyes in spite of the positively charged ZIF-8 in the membrane. This is because the negatively charged TOCN wrapped ZIF-8 to counteract the positive charge of ZIF-8. This result indicated that the obtained 21- TOCN more preferentially rejected the cationic dyes through the strong interaction with the negatively charged cellulose nanofibers (TOCN) (Varghese, Paul, & Latha, 2018). Notably, the rejection rate of the cationic dyes slightly decreased with the operating time while that of the anionic dyes rather slightly increased. This indicated that the cationic dyes were quickly adsorbed and saturated by TOCN while the anionic dyes were gradually adsorbed by ZIF-8 although TOCN disturbed their adsorptions.
## 4. Conclusion
Highly durable cellulose nanofibrous membranes with selective removal of cationic dye were successfully prepared by in-situ synthesis of ZIF-8 in TOCN dispersion. The durability of the membrane was remarkably improved by the embedded ZIF-8 as a spacer and an anchoring point of cellulose nanofibers. The morphology of the obtained composite membranes showed the cellulose nanofibers (TOCN) successfully wrapped around the ZIF-8 due to the strong charge interaction between ZIF-8 and TOCN to give well-dispersed ZIF-8 in the cellulose nanofibers. The resulted nanofiber networked structure supported by ZIF-8 could maintain the high water flux for $24\mathrm{h}$ and selectively removed the cationic dyes due to the interaction with the negative charge of the cellulose nanofibers.
